    <hr class="p6-sep" />
    <div class="p6-section-label">Critical Safety Architecture</div>
    <h2 class="p6-section-title">Overpressure & overheating protection (no single-point failure)</h2>

    <div class="p6-safety-systems-grid">
        <div class="p6-safety-system-card critical-layer">
            <div class="p6-system-badge">Layer 1 (Primary)</div>
            <h3>Mechanical Spring Relief Valve</h3>
            <ul class="p6-system-spec">
                <li><strong>Type:</strong> Direct-acting poppet valve (no electronics)</li>
                <li><strong>Setpoint:</strong> 165 PSI ±3 PSI (factory-set, tamper-evident)</li>
                <li><strong>Flow capacity:</strong> 80 L/min  full lift</li>
                <li><strong>Spring material:</strong> Inconel X (temperature stable to 400°F)</li>
                <li><strong>Pop-off time:</strong> &lt; 200 ms from 150 PSI</li>
                <li><strong>Cracking pressure (pilot):</strong> 162 PSI (prevents cycling)</li>
                <li><strong>Redundancy:</strong> Single component; no backup</li>
                <li><strong>Failure mode:</strong> Fails OPEN (safe discharge)</li>
                <li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> Bench-tested annually; replaced every 10 years</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 11px; color: #3a5a6a; border-top: 1px solid rgba(0, 255, 136, 0.2); padding-top: 0.8rem;">
                <strong>Why analog-first:</strong> Spring-loaded valves require zero electrical power
                and respond to pressure changes instantaneously. A stuck poppet at 200 PSI would only occur
                if spring is damaged (rare manufacturing defect). This layer must ALWAYS work.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p6-safety-system-card major-layer">
            <div class="p6-system-badge">Layer 2 (Thermal Override)</div>
            <h3>Boiler Thermostat Shutdown</h3>
            <ul class="p6-system-spec">
                <li><strong>Type:</strong> Bimetallic strip mechanical thermostat</li>
                <li><strong>Setpoint:</strong> 230°C (446°F) opens safety contacts</li>
                <li><strong>Hysteresis:</strong> ±3°C (prevents oscillation)</li>
                <li><strong>Response time:</strong> ~2 seconds from over-temperature trigger</li>
                <li><strong>Action:</strong> Cuts power to boiler heating relay (opens circuit)</li>
                <li><strong>Failsafe mode:</strong> If bimetal strip fails, heating element de-energizes</li>
                <li><strong>Reset:</strong> Manual twist-knob after system cools (prevents unattended restart)</li>
                <li><strong>Backup (electronic):</strong> PCW monitors boiler temperature via RTD probe</li>
                <li><strong>Dual sensing:</strong> Thermostat + RTD = redundant shutdown</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 11px; color: #3a5a6a; border-top: 1px solid rgba(0, 255, 136, 0.2); padding-top: 0.8rem;">
                <strong>Design rationale:</strong> Overheating causes boiler tube degradation and pressure
                spike. Bimetallic shutdown is independent of electrical system. Even if PCW crashes,
                mechanical thermostat kills heating.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p6-safety-system-card critical-layer">
            <div class="p6-system-badge">Layer 3 (Emergency Vent)</div>
            <h3>Battery B Emergency Pressure Bleed</h3>
            <ul class="p6-system-spec">
                <li><strong>Trigger condition:</strong> Tank pressure exceeds 155 PSI for &gt; 500 ms</li>
                <li><strong>Activation:</strong> PCW energizes normally-closed solenoid valve (Battery B powered)</li>
                <li><strong>Solenoid valve:</strong> 2-way pilot-operated dump valve</li>
                <li><strong>Vent line size:</strong> 1/2" NPT (fast discharge)</li>
                <li><strong>Discharge rate:</strong> 50 PSI → 20 PSI in ~3 seconds</li>
                <li><strong>Noise level:</strong> ~110 dB (alarm audible to driver + 30 ft radius)</li>
                <li><strong>Power consumption:</strong> 24 W continuous 12V</li>
                <li><strong>Battery B capacity:</strong> 200 Ah LiFePO₄ = 8+ hours continuous vent time</li>
                <li><strong>Manual override:</strong> Pull emergency T-handle in cabin (mechanically opens vent)</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 11px; color: #3a5a6a; border-top: 1px solid rgba(0, 255, 136, 0.2); padding-top: 0.8rem;">
                <strong>Crash scenario:</strong> If primary relief valve sticks OR boiler ruptures internally,
                this secondary vent (powered by dedicated Battery B) can reduce pressure to safe levels.
                If both batteries die, manual T-handle is accessible to driver.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p6-safety-system-card major-layer">
            <div class="p6-system-badge">Layer 4 (Rupture Prevention)</div>
            <h3>Burst Disc (Sacrificial Backup)</h3>
            <ul class="p6-system-spec">
                <li><strong>Type:</strong> Forward-acting poppet or membrane disc</li>
                <li><strong>Burst pressure:</strong> 180 PSI ±5 PSI (hardcoded, non-adjustable)</li>
                <li><strong>Material:</strong> Ductile iron (high fracture toughness)</li>
                <li><strong>Activation:</strong> Only if tank pressure reaches 180 PSI (all other layers fail)</li>
                <li><strong>Discharge method:</strong> Directs rupture energy into safe exhaust silencer</li>
                <li><strong>Replacement cycle:</strong> Every 5 years or after any over-pressure event</li>
                <li><strong>Visual indication:</strong> Disc replacement cartridge is clearly visible (service flag)</li>
                <li><strong>Fail-safe:</strong> Cannot re-seal; forces immediate maintenance</li>
                <li><strong>Cost:</strong> $35 per cartridge</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 11px; color: #3a5a6a; border-top: 1px solid rgba(0, 255, 136, 0.2); padding-top: 0.8rem;">
                <strong>Purpose:</strong> ASME code requires two independent relief devices on pressure
                vessels. Spring valve + burst disc satisfy redundancy requirement. This is true last-resort
                protection.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

