    <hr class="p6-sep" />
    <div class="p6-section-label">Impact Safety</div>
    <h2 class="p6-section-title">Boiler rupture crash scenarios & structural protection</h2>
    <p class="p6-section-lead">
        The pressure tank is the most dangerous component in a steam car. A ruptured tank at
        150 PSI releases energy equivalent to a small bomb. Phase 6 designs crash protection
        around the boiler location and analyzes impact scenarios using NHTSA frontal crash
        standards (35 mph barrier impact, 5G deceleration).
    </p>

    <div class="p6-crash-scenarios-grid">
        <div class="p6-crash-card">
            <h3>Scenario 1: Frontal Collision (35 mph barrier)</h3>
            <div class="p6-crash-spec">
                <p><strong>Impact energy:</strong> E = ½mv² = ½(2500 lbs / 32.2) × (35 mph)² ≈ 150 kJ</p>
                <p><strong>Deceleration:</strong> 5G (typical crumple zone, 0.1 s stopping time)</p>
                <p><strong>Boiler location:</strong> Mid-engine bay, behind radiator shield</p>
                <p><strong>Protective structure:</strong></p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 1.5rem; margin-top: 0.4rem;">
                    <li>• Reinforced steel bumper frame (0.25" wall thickness)</li>
                    <li>• Crumple tube honeycomb absorber (6" crush distance)</li>
                    <li>• Boiler mounted on 0.5" rubber isolation mounts (±0.5" deflection)</li>
                    <li>• Internal pressure relief valve pops at 165 PSI (safe discharge)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                    <strong>Outcome:</strong> Boiler experiences ~2G peak deceleration (safe).
                    Pressure release is slow (3–5 seconds via spring relief), no rupture. Passengers experience survivable
                    5G in cabin (modern cars: 50–60G in frontal crashes, but deceleration distributed over 0.1–0.15 s).
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="p6-crash-card">
            <h3>Scenario 2: Side Impact (Driver-Side Boiler)</h3>
            <div class="p6-crash-spec">
                <p><strong>Impact energy:</strong> NHTSA side-pole test: 31 mph impact to driver door</p>
                <p><strong>Deceleration:</strong> 8G (shorter stopping distance than frontal)</p>
                <p><strong>Boiler location:</strong> Engine centerline (NOT in side impact zone)</p>
                <p><strong>Protective structure:</strong></p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 1.5rem; margin-top: 0.4rem;">
                    <li>• Boiler is 30" forward of driver position</li>
                    <li>• Door intrusion limit: 6" (boiler outside cabin intrusion envelope)</li>
                    <li>• Side-impact bar in door frame isolates cabin from boiler zone</li>
                    <li>• Boiler sits on shock absorbers (lateral compliance)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                    <strong>Outcome:</strong> Boiler is physically separated from impact zone.
                    Direct driver-side impact unlikely to affect boiler. Side-door pillar protects cabin occupants from boiler
                    if it does move.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="p6-crash-card">
            <h3>Scenario 3: Rear-End Collision (35 mph rear impact)</h3>
            <div class="p6-crash-spec">
                <p><strong>Impact energy:</strong> Vehicle struck from rear at 35 mph</p>
                <p><strong>Deceleration:</strong> 4G (longer crumple zone in rear)</p>
                <p><strong>Boiler location:</strong> Mid-engine, NOT in rear impact zone</p>
                <p><strong>Protective structure:</strong></p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 1.5rem; margin-top: 0.4rem;">
                    <li>• Rear bumper/crumple absorber: 8" crush distance</li>
                    <li>• Transmission transmission & driveshaft flex absorbs energy</li>
                    <li>• Boiler is 48" forward of rear bumper (safe distance)</li>
                    <li>• No sharp discontinuities in frame behind boiler</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                    <strong>Outcome:</strong> Rear impact energy dissipated in rear crumple zone.
                    Boiler unaffected. Vehicle push-forward may cause slight pressure increase, but relief valve pop-off at 165 PSI
                    prevents rupture.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="p6-crash-card">
            <h3>Scenario 4: Rollover (Worst-Case Boiler Impact)</h3>
            <div class="p6-crash-spec">
                <p><strong>Event:</strong> Vehicle rolls, boiler impacts ground at 45° angle</p>
                <p><strong>Impact velocity:</strong> ~25 mph (roof velocity during 3/4 roll cycle)</p>
                <p><strong>Peak deceleration:</strong> 10G+ (ground impact is sudden)</p>
                <p><strong>Protective structure:</strong></p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 1.5rem; margin-top: 0.4rem;">
                    <li>• Boiler encased in 0.375" steel cage (tubing, welded frame)</li>
                    <li>• Cage attached to frame via energy-absorbing mounts (elastomer + damper)</li>
                    <li>• Boiler internal safety systems engage (spring relief + burst disc)</li>
                    <li>• Pressure release: 2–3 seconds to safe levels (steam vents through cage openings)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                    <strong>Outcome:</strong> High-G impact may exceed spring relief setpoint tolerance,
                    but burst disc (180 PSI setpoint) activates. Controlled rupture through exhaust silencer. Boiler cage prevents
                    projectile motion and redirects steam away from cabin. Occupants experience high G's but are protected by
                    rollover protection system (ROPS) cage.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

